    "lawCode": "Amd §40, Chap 266 of 1986",
    "lawSection": "Insurance",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6026\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend chapter 266 of the laws of 1986, amending the civil\npractice law and rules and other laws relating to malpractice and\nprofessional medical conduct, in relation to the effectiveness thereof\n\n\n PURPOSE\n\nThis bill would extend for one more year the freeze on medical\nmalpractice premium rates for physicians and surgeons, and prevent a\nsurcharge on premiums, until June 30, 2010.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends Chapter 266 of the Laws of 1986 to\nprovide that between July 1, 2009 and June 30, 2010, the\nSuperintendent of Insurance (\"Superintendent\") shall not: (1) increase\nmedical malpractice insurance rates for physicians and surgeons; and\n(2) impose a surcharge on premiums to satisfy a deficiency\nattributable to established premium rates for prior years.\n\nSection 2 of the bill provides that this bill would take effect\nimmediately.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nThe Superintendent currently establishes premium rates for medical\nmalpractice insurance for physicians and surgeons on an annual basis.\nThe law requires the Superintendent to impose a surcharge on premiums\nto satisfy a projected deficiency attributable to established premium\nlevels for prior years. The Superintendent of Insurance is not\npermitted by law to establish or approve a rate increase or impose a\nsurcharge on premiums until June 30, 2009.\n\n STATEMENT IN SUPPORT :\n\nCurrently, the Executive, the Legislature and stakeholders are engaged\nin discussions to develop long-term solutions to the medical\nmalpractice problem confronting the state. The aim of these\ndiscussions is to provide premium relief for physicians, enhance\npatient safety and stabilize the medical malpractice insurance sector.\nHowever, the Superintendent is required to evaluate the adequacy of\nrates and the necessity for imposing a surcharge on premiums. This\ncould lead to a significant premium increase even while these\nimportant discussions proceed.\n\nThis bill would stabilize premiums for physicians and prevent a\nsurcharge on premiums until June 30, 2010, while providing the\nExecutive, the Legislature and stakeholders with additional time to\ndevelop long-term solutions to this complex problem. Doing so would\nalso avoid a significant financial burden on physicians in the\nshort-term.\n\n BUDGET IMPLICATIONS :\n\nThis bill would not have an impact on state finances.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill would take effect immediately.\n",
